    "created_at": "2026-11-15T18:38:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-15T18:38:59Z",
    "body": "This is now implemented in #13100!\n\n> Notes from WHATNOT: we're going to pursue/research adding _all_ of the options:\n> \n> * `<template>` inside `<svg>` or `<mathml>` inherits the ancestor namespace\n\nOne design question that I had to answer about the above is whether this inherited namespace is \"sticky\" - as in, what happens when a template is parsed in one context and then moved to another:\n\n```html\n<svg>\n  <!-- originalMarkup is parsed as SVG -->\n  <template id=\"myTemplate\">{originalMarkup}</template>\n</svg>\n<script>\n  // myTemplate will now have an HTML ancestor\n  document.body.append(myTemplate);\n  // Is newMarkup parsed as HTML or SVG?\n  myTemplate.setHTML(newMarkup);\n</script>\n```\n\nIn #13100, the template's inherited namespace is a parser-time piece of state - so the answer to the above question is \"html\".\nBut it's not necessarily obvious.\n\nIn general, the inherited namespace case is the one with the most gotchas and I wonder if it's needed at all.\n\n> * A successful `<template for=...>` inherits the namespace from the target\n> * `<template type=\"html|mathml|svg\">` allows doing this explicitly\n\nThis problem doesn't apply to the above because the template is not referable (a burn-once `<template for>`) or has an explicit namespace.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
